= Courses
Course information, lecture slides, and materials

Materials are updated during the semester. The files below are placeholders -- replace them
with your own PDFs in the +www+ +files+ folder (see GUIDE.md).

== Example Course 1 -- Wireless Communications

~~~
{Course information}
- *Code*: EE301 \M *Level*: Undergraduate \M *Semester*: Spring 2026 \M *Credits*: 3
- *When*: Monday 09:00--11:00, Building B1, Room 201
- *Office hours*: Tuesday 14:00--16:00, or by appointment

*What you will learn:* how a wireless link works, from path loss and fading to the capacity of
a multi-user system; and how to formulate and solve a simple resource allocation problem.
~~~

*Materials*

- [files/syllabus.pdf Syllabus and reading list (PDF)]
- [files/lecture-01.pdf Lecture 1 -- Introduction and path loss (PDF)]
- [files/lecture-02.pdf Lecture 2 -- Fading and diversity (PDF)]
- [files/lecture-03.pdf Lecture 3 -- Capacity and resource allocation (PDF)]

== Example Course 2 -- Convex Optimization for Engineers

~~~
{Course information}
- *Code*: EE502 \M *Level*: Graduate \M *Semester*: Autumn 2025 \M *Credits*: 3
- *When*: Wednesday 14:00--16:00, Building B1, Room 405

*What you will learn:* convex sets and functions, duality, and how to recognise and solve the
optimization problems that appear in communications and signal processing.
~~~

*Materials*

- [files/syllabus.pdf Syllabus and reading list (PDF)]
- [files/lecture-01.pdf Lecture 1 -- Convex sets and functions (PDF)]

== Example Course 3 -- Research Methods Seminar

~~~
{Course information}
- *Level*: Master and PhD \M *Semester*: Every semester \M *Credits*: 1

*What you will learn:* how to read a paper critically, how to write a technical report, how to
give a seminar talk, and how to use Git and LaTeX for your thesis.
~~~

*Materials*

- [files/syllabus.pdf Seminar plan and schedule (PDF)]

